 ## Supported Backends
 
 - ARM NN (Mali)
+- ggml (any Vulkan 1.2+ GPU, e.g. through Mesa; CLIP image embeddings only)
 - CUDA (NVIDIA GPUs with [compute capability](https://developer.nvidia.com/cuda-gpus) 5.2 or higher)
 - ROCm (AMD GPUs)
 - OpenVINO (Intel GPUs such as Iris Xe and Arc)
@@ -21,6 +22,7 @@ You do not need to redo any machine learning jobs after enabling hardware accele
 - Only Linux and Windows (through WSL2) servers are supported.
 - ARM NN is only supported on devices with Mali GPUs. Other Arm devices are not supported.
 - Some models may not be compatible with certain backends. CUDA is the most reliable.
+- ggml only accelerates CLIP image embeddings (Smart Search jobs). Text search, facial recognition and OCR stay on the CPU.
 - Search latency isn't improved by ARM NN due to model compatibility issues preventing its use. However, smart search jobs do make use of ARM NN.
 
 ## Prerequisites
@@ -38,6 +40,13 @@ You do not need to redo any machine learning jobs after enabling hardware accele
 - Optional: Configure your `.env` file, see [environment variables](/install/environment-variables) for ARM NN specific settings
   - In particular, the `MACHINE_LEARNING_ANN_FP16_TURBO` can significantly improve performance at the cost of very slightly lower accuracy
 
+#### ggml
+
+- A Vulkan driver for your GPU on the host (`/dev/dri` must be available). The image ships Mesa's drivers; for other drivers, bind-mount the driver's ICD file and library and set `VK_DRIVER_FILES` accordingly
+- CLIP models are converted from ONNX to GGUF on first load and cached next to the ONNX file
+- If the GPU is shared with a latency-sensitive workload (e.g. an NVR's object detector), set `GGML_VK_MAX_NODES_PER_SUBMIT=1` in the container's environment: the encoder then submits one graph node at a time so other jobs can interleave, at about 1-2% extra latency
+- Optional: `MACHINE_LEARNING_GGML_PRECISION=FP16` roughly halves latency on GPUs with fp16 support at a very small accuracy cost, `MACHINE_LEARNING_GGML_WEIGHT_TYPE=q8_0` halves GPU memory. See [environment variables](/install/environment-variables)
+
 #### CUDA
 
 - The GPU must have compute capability 5.2 or greater.
@@ -88,7 +97,7 @@ You do not need to redo any machine learning jobs after enabling hardware accele
 ## Setup
 
 1. If you do not already have it, download the latest [`hwaccel.ml.yml`][hw-file] file and ensure it's in the same folder as the `docker-compose.yml`.
-2. In `immich-machine-learning`, add one of -[armnn, cuda, rocm, openvino, rknn] to the `image` section's tag at the end of the line.
+2. In `immich-machine-learning`, add one of -[armnn, cuda, ggml, rocm, openvino, rknn] to the `image` section's tag at the end of the line.
 3. Still in the `docker-compose.yml` under `immich-machine-learning`, uncomment the `extends` section and change `cpu` to the appropriate backend.
 4. Redeploy the `immich-machine-learning` container with these updated settings.
 
@@ -96,7 +105,7 @@ You do not need to redo any machine learning jobs after enabling hardware accele
 
 You can confirm the device is being recognized and used by checking its utilization. There are many tools to display this, such as `nvtop` for NVIDIA or Intel, `intel_gpu_top` for Intel, and `radeontop` for AMD.
 
-You can also check the logs of the `immich-machine-learning` container. When a Smart Search or Face Detection job begins, or when you search with text in Immich, you should either see a log for `Available ORT providers` containing the relevant provider (e.g. `CUDAExecutionProvider` in the case of CUDA), or a `Loaded ANN model` log entry without errors in the case of ARM NN.
+You can also check the logs of the `immich-machine-learning` container. When a Smart Search or Face Detection job begins, or when you search with text in Immich, you should either see a log for `Available ORT providers` containing the relevant provider (e.g. `CUDAExecutionProvider` in the case of CUDA), a `Loaded ANN model` log entry without errors in the case of ARM NN, or a `Loaded ggml model on Vulkan0` entry in the case of ggml.
 
